{"qid":"Q335088","names":["Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia"],"title":"Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia","endonym":{"native":"Կիլիկիայի Հայկական Թագավորություն","lang":"hy"},"capsule":"The Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia was established in 1198 when Leo I (Leo the Magnificent) received a royal crown from Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI and Pope Celestine III, elevating the Rubenid principality — founded c. 1080 by refugees fleeing Seljuq conquest — to a kingdom centred on Sis (modern Kozan, Turkey). Positioned at the intersection of Crusader states and the Byzantine and Islamic worlds, Cilician Armenia was a key commercial and military ally of the Crusades, with its ports at Ayas and Tarsus handling substantial east-west trade. The kingdom weathered Mongol overlordship from the 1240s by treaty accommodation, but repeated Mamluk invasions progressively reduced its territory; the final blow came when the Mamluk Sultan Barquq's forces captured and sacked Sis in 1375, killing the last king, Leo V's line in exile, and ending three centuries of Armenian statehood in Cilicia.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Cilician Armenia's territorial extent is moderately well attested through Armenian, Crusader, and Arabic chronicles and has been mapped by historians; precise frontier zones in the Taurus Mountains and with neighbouring Crusader states are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_Kingdom_of_Cilicia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Leo I of Armenian Cilicia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_I_of_Armenian_Cilicia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: